{"region": "asia", "note": "The Eras level. Sourced areas carry the cited periods of a published periodization (cite); elsewhere, and after an area's last period, the region's period bins per lane group (basis: period-bin), kept only where the data records a state or culture. Leaders are computed from the spans: most lane-years, weighted by tier.", "areas": [{"id": "japan", "label": "Japan", "lanes": ["japan"], "note": "The Jōmon and Yayoi periods from the list of archaeological periods, then the periods of Japanese history as headed in History of Japan, from the Kofun period to Reiwa. The list calls c. 250 to 710 the Yamato period; the article divides it into Kofun and Asuka, which are used here.", "sources": [{"page": "History of Japan", "revid": 1379411393, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Japan", "permalink": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1379411393"}, {"page": "List of archaeological periods", "revid": 1360612640, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_archaeological_periods", "permalink": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1360612640"}]}, {"id": "korea", "label": "Korea", "lanes": ["north-korea", "south-korea"], "note": "The four periods the list of archaeological periods gives for Korea from the Jeulmun pottery period on (its Paleolithic ends in 8000 BCE, before the chart starts). The list dates the boundary between the Protohistoric period and the Three Kingdoms \"300/400 CE\"; both readings are kept, the Protohistoric period running to 400 and the Three Kingdoms starting in 300, so the two share the width for those 100 years. After 668 the region's period bins take over.", "sources": [{"page": "List of archaeological periods", "revid": 1360612640, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_archaeological_periods", "permalink": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1360612640"}]}, {"id": "china", "label": "China", "lanes": ["china"], "note": "The Neolithic period and Ancient China of the list of archaeological periods' China row. Its Imperial period (221 BCE to 1911) is not used: the region's period bins divide those years at the Qin, Sui and Mongol dates and the First Opium War, which says more, and its Modern period has no dates. The Neolithic period begins about 10,000 BCE and is clipped to the start of the chart.", "sources": [{"page": "List of archaeological periods", "revid": 1360612640, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_archaeological_periods", "permalink": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1360612640"}]}, {"id": "group-south-asia", "label": null, "group": "south-asia", "lanes": ["afghanistan", "pakistan", "india", "nepal", "bhutan", "bangladesh", "sri-lanka"], "bins": true}, {"id": "group-caucasus", "label": null, "group": "caucasus", "lanes": ["georgia", "armenia", "azerbaijan"], "bins": true}, {"id": "group-central-asia", "label": null, "group": "central-asia", "lanes": ["kazakhstan", "turkmenistan", "uzbekistan", "kyrgyzstan", "tajikistan"], "bins": true}, {"id": "group-mainland-southeast", "label": null, "group": "mainland-southeast", "lanes": ["myanmar", "thailand", "laos", "cambodia", "vietnam"], "bins": true}, {"id": "group-maritime-southeast", "label": null, "group": "maritime-southeast", "lanes": ["malaysia", "singapore", "brunei", "indonesia", "timor-leste", "philippines"], "bins": true}, {"id": "group-east-asia", "label": null, "group": "east-asia", "lanes": ["mongolia", "china", "taiwan", "north-korea", "south-korea", "japan"], "bins": true}, {"id": "group-north-asia", "label": null, "group": "north-asia", "lanes": ["russia-east"], "bins": true}],
